00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
this school in tripoli will soon become  a polling station  wednesday is a deadline for registered  voters to pick up their voter cards  ahead of the first round of presidential  elections scheduled for december 24th  it will be the first presidential  election in libya's history and more  than two million people have received  their voter cards so far  but the vote has been marred by  controversy some say holding  presidential elections without a  constitution will only plunge the  country into further violence  this crowd has gathered in front of the  election commission headquarters calling  for the vote to be postponed until a  constitutional referendum takes place  without a constitutional foundation  in any nation in the world you cannot  conduct a proper and neutral and just  election this is abcd it is the very  beginning lesson in democracy you have  to have a constitution which regulates  respect identify the identity of the  country for this election this election  proposed election it is very dangerous  for democracy very dangerous for a civil  state and we think personally speaking  that this election is not going to end  in a peaceful manner  libya has been engulfed in violence and  political division since the revolution  that toppled walmart gaddafi in 2011.  with warlord khalifa hafter controlling  the oil-rich region of eastern libya  and the internationally recognized  government of unity in the west led by  prime minister abdul hamid daba both men  are among scores of candidates running  in the elections  across libya people have mixed feelings  in my opinion presidential elections  right now should not take place you  shouldn't have presidential elections  before the parliamentary elections and  there should be a constitution  i'm in favor of these elections at least  the situation will get better we want  our country to move forward in my  opinion it's the only way to end the  violence  just three weeks ahead of the vote  libyans are still deeply divided there's  international pressure for the elections  to move ahead as scheduled but without a  constitution in place many here feel  presidential elections will only prolong  the conflict mala trainer al jazeera  tripoli
 

